From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Subject: b43: dma: map RX buffers bidirectionally so the RX poison works

b43 poisons every RX buffer before handing it to the device (frame length
0, 0xff bytes behind the frame offset) and drops buffers that still carry
the poison: the device sometimes skips a buffer, and dma_rx() relies on
the poison to recognise it.

The RX buffers are mapped and synced DMA_FROM_DEVICE. On a non-coherent
ARM system (BCM4708/BCM4709, Cortex-A9 + PL310) dma_map_single() and
dma_sync_single_for_device() then only invalidate the cache, so the
poison the CPU just wrote is discarded and never reaches memory. A
skipped buffer then holds stale memory instead: the plaintext of earlier
TX frames, or an old frame from the previous use of that buffer. Seen on
a Netgear R6400 (BCM4331): garbage "frame lengths" between normal frames,
buffers full of the router's own transmitted download payload, an RX ring
that lost sync and hung the router, and old frames handed up again.

Map, unmap and sync the RX buffers DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL: the poison is
written back on map/sync-for-device and the cache is invalidated on
sync-for-cpu. After this, skipped buffers are recognised and dropped
(about 150 per hour under load on the R6400) and no garbage reaches the
RX path any more.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/dma.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/dma.c
index e0a74f4..d842cbd 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/dma.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/dma.c
@@ -353,8 +353,16 @@ static inline
 		dmaaddr = dma_map_single(ring->dev->dev->dma_dev,
 					 buf, len, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 	} else {
+		/*
+		 * Bidirectional, not FROM_DEVICE: on non-coherent ARM a
+		 * FROM_DEVICE map/sync only invalidates the cache, so the
+		 * poison written by b43_poison_rx_buffer() never reached
+		 * memory. A buffer the device skipped then held stale data
+		 * (e.g. an old TX frame) instead of the poison and was read
+		 * as a frame with an impossible length.
+		 */
 		dmaaddr = dma_map_single(ring->dev->dev->dma_dev,
-					 buf, len, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
+					 buf, len, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 	}
 
 	return dmaaddr;
@@ -369,7 +377,7 @@ static inline
 				 addr, len, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 	} else {
 		dma_unmap_single(ring->dev->dev->dma_dev,
-				 addr, len, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
+				 addr, len, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 	}
 }
 
@@ -379,7 +387,7 @@ static inline
 {
 	B43_WARN_ON(ring->tx);
 	dma_sync_single_for_cpu(ring->dev->dev->dma_dev,
-				    addr, len, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
+				    addr, len, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 }
 
 static inline
@@ -388,7 +396,7 @@ static inline
 {
 	B43_WARN_ON(ring->tx);
 	dma_sync_single_for_device(ring->dev->dev->dma_dev,
-				   addr, len, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
+				   addr, len, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 }
 
 static inline
